The cause is explicit in the mod: IsoTree is deliberately included in the eligibility allowlist.
At [HoverHighlighting.lua (line 275)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/mod_projects/hoverhighlighting/hoverhighlighting/42/media/lua/client/HoverHighlighting.lua:275), contributesWorldMenu() classifies interaction sources. Its final class list contains:
or instanceof(obj, "IsoTree")
at [line 311 (line 311)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/mod_projects/hoverhighlighting/hoverhighlighting/42/media/lua/client/HoverHighlighting.lua:311). Therefore every genuine IsoTree passes isEligible().
It can then enter the highlight set through either path:
- A directly picked tree passes pickIsVisible()—which also explicitly exempts IsoTree from the normal seen-square requirement—then isEligible() at [lines 699–731 (line 699)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/mod_projects/hoverhighlighting/hoverhighlighting/42/media/lua/client/HoverHighlighting.lua:699).
- The recovery scan visits nearby squares, checks every eligible object whose sprite mask contains the mouse, and adds it at [lines 602–615 (line 602)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/mod_projects/hoverhighlighting/hoverhighlighting/42/media/lua/client/HoverHighlighting.lua:602). That scan extends up to three squares toward the camera specifically to find tall sprites at [lines 659–689 (line 659)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/mod_projects/hoverhighlighting/hoverhighlighting/42/media/lua/client/HoverHighlighting.lua:659).
The reason IsoTree was probably included is that vanilla treats it as a world-menu source: it records an IsoTree as tree, then offers “Chop Tree” when the player possesses a usable item tagged CHOP_TREE, in [ISWorldObjectContextMenuLogic.java (line 1927)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/reference/zomboid_decompiled/iso/ISWorldObjectContextMenuLogic.java:1927). HoverHighlighting only checks the object class, however—it does not reproduce vanilla’s axe/inventory condition. Thus a tree highlights even when “Chop Tree” would not currently appear.
The inspections do not actually show a tree object. The last two relevant outdoor blocks in [console.txt (line 817)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/mod_projects/hoverhighlighting/console.txt:817) and [console.txt (line 823)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/mod_projects/hoverhighlighting/console.txt:823) contain only:
- a ground IsoObject;
- optionally an object named Grass.
A genuine IsoTree:getObjectName() returns "Tree" in [IsoTree.java (line 173)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/reference/zomboid_decompiled/iso/objects/IsoTree.java:173). The UnfinishedBusiness inspector logs only square:getObjects() for the selected square at [Locations.lua (line 196)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/mod_projects/unfinishedBusiness/unfinishedbusiness/unfinishedbusiness/42/media/lua/client/UnfinishedBusiness/Locations.lua:196). Consequently, those inspections either targeted foliage that is visually tree-like but is actually Grass, or targeted a ground square covered by a tall tree whose actual anchor is on a neighboring square. HoverHighlighting’s nearby-square scan can still find that neighboring anchor.
So the likely highlighted/non-highlighted distinction is:
- Highlighting: genuine IsoTree, explicitly allowed.
- Not highlighting: visually similar vegetation represented as Grass or another generic IsoObject.
The packaged Workshop copy also contains the same IsoTree eligibility check, so this is not a source-versus-deployment discrepancy. I made no changes. If trees should never highlight, the narrow change would be to remove them from contributesWorldMenu(); alternatively, tree eligibility could be made conditional on vanilla’s actual Chop Tree prerequisites.
