Yes—Build 42.21 provides two practical ways to expose a customizable keybinding. Both use the game’s existing key-capture dialog.
Available approaches
Approach	Appears under	Persistence	Runtime lookup	Modifiers
Vanilla keyBinding registry	Options → Key Bindings	keysB42.ini	isKeyDown("bindingName")	Supported
PZAPI.ModOptions:addKeyBind	Options → Mods → Hover Highlighting	ModOptions.ini	isKeyDown(option:getValue())	Not reliably persisted
Custom selector UI	Wherever we build it	Mod-owned	Mod-owned	Entirely custom


1. Vanilla Key Bindings page
The vanilla Key Bindings page is generated from the global keyBinding table. MainOptions.loadKeys() reads every entry, registers it with the Java Core, and loads player overrides from keysB42.ini at [MainOptions.lua (line 3472)](/home/red5/.local/share/Steam/steamapps/common/ProjectZomboid/projectzomboid/media/lua/client/OptionScreens/MainOptions.lua:3472).
This table can be extended. Vanilla Foraging does exactly that during OnGameBoot:
table.insert(keyBinding, { value = "[Search Mode]" })
table.insert(keyBinding, {
    value = KeybindId.TOGGLE_SEARCH_MODE:getId(),
    key = 207
})
See [ISSearchManager.lua (line 1472)](/home/red5/.local/share/Steam/steamapps/common/ProjectZomboid/projectzomboid/media/lua/client/Foraging/ISSearchManager.lua:1472).
HoverHighlighting could use the same pattern with a unique string identifier, without creating a KeybindId:
table.insert(keyBinding, { value = "[HoverHighlighting]" })
table.insert(keyBinding, {
    value = "HoverHighlightingActivation",
    key = Keyboard.KEY_NONE
})
Translations would be resolved as:
UI_optionscreen_binding_HoverHighlighting
UI_optionscreen_binding_HoverHighlightingActivation
The page-building code turns category entries into headings and ordinary entries into selector buttons at [MainOptions.lua (line 2148)](/home/red5/.local/share/Steam/steamapps/common/ProjectZomboid/projectzomboid/media/lua/client/OptionScreens/MainOptions.lua:2148).
When Apply or Accept is pressed, the game:
- writes the assignment to keysB42.ini;
- registers it through getCore():addKeyBinding(...);
- preserves the main key, alternate key, and modifier flags.
That flow is at [MainOptions.lua (line 3706)](/home/red5/.local/share/Steam/steamapps/common/ProjectZomboid/projectzomboid/media/lua/client/OptionScreens/MainOptions.lua:3706).
Runtime use would then be simply:
isKeyDown("HoverHighlightingActivation")
The string overload resolves the registered Core.KeyBinding and honors its Shift/Ctrl/Alt requirements in [GameKeyboard.java (line 139)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/reference/zomboid_decompiled/input/GameKeyboard.java:139) and [Core.java (line 2767)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/reference/zomboid_decompiled/core/Core.java:2767).
This also gets the vanilla duplicate-binding dialog, Clear and Default buttons, keyboard keys, and mouse buttons. The selector itself captures keyboard and mouse input at [ISSetKeybindDialog.lua (line 85)](/home/red5/.local/share/Steam/steamapps/common/ProjectZomboid/projectzomboid/media/lua/client/ISUI/ISSetKeybindDialog.lua:85).
The trade-off is that keyBinding is a vanilla global integration surface rather than a named mod API. Nevertheless, vanilla itself demonstrates the extension pattern.
2. Mod Options keybind
PZAPI.ModOptions already has a keybinding control:
local activationKeyOption = options:addKeyBind(
    "activationKey",
    "IGUI_HoverHighlighting_ActivationKey",
    Keyboard.KEY_NONE,
    "IGUI_HoverHighlighting_ActivationKey_tt"
)
The API is defined at [ModOptions.lua (line 182)](/home/red5/.local/share/Steam/steamapps/common/ProjectZomboid/projectzomboid/media/lua/client/PZAPI/ModOptions.lua:182).
It appears beside the existing color option under Options → Mods → Hover Highlighting. It uses the same vanilla key-capture dialog and participates in duplicate-binding detection because its control is added to MainOptions.keyText at [MainOptions.lua (line 2987)](/home/red5/.local/share/Steam/steamapps/common/ProjectZomboid/projectzomboid/media/lua/client/OptionScreens/MainOptions.lua:2987).
Its numeric key code is persisted in ModOptions.ini at [ModOptions.lua (line 259)](/home/red5/.local/share/Steam/steamapps/common/ProjectZomboid/projectzomboid/media/lua/client/PZAPI/ModOptions.lua:259) and restored at [ModOptions.lua (line 292)](/home/red5/.local/share/Steam/steamapps/common/ProjectZomboid/projectzomboid/media/lua/client/PZAPI/ModOptions.lua:292).
Runtime use would be:
isKeyDown(activationKeyOption:getValue())
Numeric key checking also supports mouse-button codes: values at or above 10000 are routed to the mouse state at [GameKeyboard.java (line 129)](/home/red5/Projects/project_zomboid/reference/zomboid_decompiled/input/GameKeyboard.java:129).
The limitation is modifier combinations. The capture dialog recognizes Shift/Ctrl/Alt, but PZAPI.ModOptions:save() stores only option.key, not the modifier flags. Therefore this route should be treated as a single keyboard key or mouse button selector.
3. Custom selector
A completely custom selector could reuse ISSetKeybindDialog or capture key events itself, but there is no advantage here. It would duplicate selection, conflict checking, key naming, mouse handling, persistence, and reset behavior already supplied by the two existing systems.
Recommendation
Use the vanilla keyBinding registry if the desired UX is specifically:
Options → Key Bindings → Hover Highlighting → Activation Key

It is the better keybinding implementation because it provides normal game persistence, conflict handling, Clear/Default behavior, mouse buttons, and modifier combinations. Registering it from a client file during OnGameBoot follows the same lifecycle pattern as vanilla Search Mode.
Keep the activation-mode checkbox in the existing Hover Highlighting Mod Options page. That means the setting and binding live on different tabs, but each is stored by the game system best suited to it.
If keeping both controls together is more important than modifier support, options:addKeyBind() is the simpler alternative and integrates naturally with the existing color-picker setup.
For the render guard, releasing or clearing the activation key must call clearAllHighlights() before returning. A bare early return would leave the last highlighted objects active:
if activationModeOption:getValue()
    and not isKeyDown("HoverHighlightingActivation")
then
    clearAllHighlights()
    return
end
